From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: ASoC: sunxi: h713-msp: a wake-up call the DSP is late for is asked again, not an aborted download

After a standby the audio DSP is rebuilt (h713-mips-restart writes "reset"),
and in 3 of 20 wakes that took a second or a third attempt (TODO 2.37). Measured 12.10.2026 with the selftest and the
wake-up call instrumented (arbeit/wurzeln-20261012, 21 + 12 wakes): the
island comes up and the selftest answers at once every time; what failed
was the one read of 0x00EE at a block boundary of the patch download
(msp_kick), which ran into the mailbox's 50 ms, and a timed-out read there
aborted the whole download -- island reset, "attempt 2". With the read
repeated, the DSP answered the next request at once, both times ("after
50 ms and 1 timed-out read", blocks 0 and 4): at a block boundary it is
busy with the block it was just given and sometimes takes longer than 50 ms
to take a request.

msp_kick() has its own deadline of 500 ms for exactly this -- a DSP that is
slow to say 0x0180 -- but the single read inside it was not allowed to be
slow. Now a read the DSP does not answer is asked again up to that deadline,
quietly (not a mailbox timeout, not counted as one), with one line when it
happened; a DSP that answers nothing in 500 ms is one that is not running,
and that remains the error that resets the island. The three-attempt loop
stays as the net.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Mythos 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
@@ -582,7 +582,11 @@
  * written again. Never during the download: there the one wake-up read per
  * block must stay one read, so a dropped request is left to the timeout.
  */
-static int msp_dsp_read(struct h713_msp *msp, u16 addr, u16 *val)
+/*
+ * @quiet: a timeout is the caller's to judge -- not counted, not warned
+ * about. The wake-up call at a block boundary (msp_kick()) asks again.
+ */
+static int __msp_dsp_read(struct h713_msp *msp, u16 addr, u16 *val, bool quiet)
 {
 	bool pending_seen = false;
 	unsigned int spins = 0, reissues = 0;
@@ -591,7 +595,8 @@
 
 	ret = msp_wait_status(msp, MSP_ST_DSP1_WBUSY, false);
 	if (ret) {
-		msp_note_timeout(msp, "read (busy)", addr);
+		if (!quiet)
+			msp_note_timeout(msp, "read (busy)", addr);
 		return ret;
 	}
 
@@ -621,7 +626,8 @@
 		}
 
 		if (ktime_after(ktime_get(), deadline)) {
-			msp_note_timeout(msp, "read", addr);
+			if (!quiet)
+				msp_note_timeout(msp, "read", addr);
 			return -ETIMEDOUT;
 		}
 
@@ -639,6 +645,11 @@
 	}
 }
 
+static int msp_dsp_read(struct h713_msp *msp, u16 addr, u16 *val)
+{
+	return __msp_dsp_read(msp, addr, val, false);
+}
+
 static int msp_dsp_update(struct h713_msp *msp, u16 addr, u16 mask, u16 val)
 {
 	u16 old;
@@ -652,22 +663,46 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * The wake-up call of plan section 3.3: exactly one read of 0x00EE at a
- * block boundary, repeated until the monitor answers 0x0180. mbx.c -K notes
- * a stubborn answer and carries on rather than aborting, and so does this --
- * the download itself decides success, not this poll.
+ * The wake-up call of plan section 3.3: a read of 0x00EE at a block boundary,
+ * repeated until the monitor answers 0x0180. mbx.c -K notes a stubborn answer
+ * and carries on rather than aborting, and so does this -- the download
+ * itself decides success, not this poll.
+ *
+ * A read the DSP does not answer within the mailbox's 50 ms is asked again,
+ * up to this call's own deadline, and is no error here: at a block boundary
+ * the DSP is busy with the block it was just given, and in 3 of 20 wakes from
+ * standby (and 1 of 20 cold boots) it took longer than 50 ms to take the
+ * request -- the next request was answered at once every time (12.10.2026,
+ * arbeit/wurzeln-20261012: "after 50 ms and 1 timed-out read", blocks 0 and
+ * 4). Until then that one timeout aborted the whole download and the island
+ * was reset and rebuilt, the "attempt 2" and "attempt 3" of TODO 2.37 -- a
+ * failure this call's deadline was meant to absorb. A DSP that answers
+ * nothing within the deadline is one that is not running, and that is the
+ * error.
  */
 static int msp_kick(struct h713_msp *msp, int block)
 {
-	ktime_t deadline = ktime_add_ms(ktime_get(), MSP_KICK_TIMEOUT_MS);
-	unsigned int reads = 0;
+	ktime_t t0 = ktime_get(), deadline = ktime_add_ms(t0, MSP_KICK_TIMEOUT_MS);
+	unsigned int reads = 0, late = 0;
 	u16 val;
 	int ret;
 
 	for (;;) {
-		ret = msp_dsp_read(msp, DSP_CLOCK, &val);
-		if (ret)
-			return ret;
+		ret = __msp_dsp_read(msp, DSP_CLOCK, &val, true);
+		if (ret) {
+			late++;
+			if (ktime_after(ktime_get(), deadline)) {
+				dev_err(msp->dev,
+					"Wake-up call after block %d: 0x00EE not answered in %lld ms (%u requests)\n",
+					block, ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), t0), late);
+				return ret;
+			}
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (late)
+			dev_info(msp->dev,
+				 "Wake-up call after block %d: answered after %lld ms, %u request%s unanswered before\n",
+				 block, ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), t0), late, late == 1 ? "" : "s");
 		reads++;
 		if (val == DSP_ALIVE_ANSWER)
 			return 0;
